carlos-ctl (1.2.0~dev1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * db-users creates carlos_report, the read-only account carlos-emr's
    Query by Example now runs as (carlos-emr/carlos#3372), records it as
    db_reporting_username/db_reporting_password in carlos.properties, and
    applies the grant list the deployed WAR ships
    (WEB-INF/classes/db/reporting/carlos_report_grants.sql): SELECT on each
    reportable table that exists, minus hidden columns, and nothing else.
    db-migrate applies the list again after every successful migration, so
    a reportable table a migration adds is granted once it exists; a list
    that does not apply is reported and never fails the migration. rotate
    rotates the new password with the others.
  * check reports the account: present on exactly its two hosts, named in
    carlos.properties, able to log in with the recorded password, and
    granted on each host exactly what the deployed list allows (every
    reportable table, every permitted column of a table with hidden
    columns; no restricted or unclassified table, hidden column,
    table-wide grant on a table with hidden columns, write privilege,
    role, PROXY grant, or PUBLIC grant reaching the schema, routines
    included).
  * db-users refreshes the grants when it cannot count the schema's tables
    (with a warning) rather than skipping them, asks for a restart when it
    corrects the recorded reporting username, and an unreadable or
    non-UTF-8 grant list is reported instead of raising.
  * Before applying the grant list (in db-users and after db-migrate), drop
    any carlos_report login for a host other than localhost and 127.0.0.1,
    because the list grants every login the account has. An operator's
    carlos_report@'%' would otherwise get the clinical tables behind a
    password carlos-ctl does not manage. If such a login cannot be dropped,
    the list is not applied.
  * destroy-data drops carlos_report with the other accounts.
  * db-users holds the database-ownership lock that finish-install,
    import-o19 and both postinsts share, from before it reads the recorded
    passwords until it has written them, so two runs (a first-run postinst
    and a hand-run rotate, say) can no longer leave carlos.properties
    recording one password while MariaDB accepts another. A caller already
    holding the lock (a postinst on fd 9, finish-install in-process) is let
    through; while another run holds it, db-users refuses and changes
    nothing. A run stopped between its ALTER USERs and its writes is
    repaired by the next db-users, which sets MariaDB back to the recorded
    passwords.

 -- CARLOS EMR Maintainers <michael@maplecreekmedical.ca>  Thu, 22 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

carlos-ctl (1.1.2~dev1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * import-o19 now takes the database-ownership lock
    (/var/lib/carlos-emr/.finish-install.lock) that carlos-emr's and
    carlos-emr-drugref's configure, carlos-emr-provision.service and
    finish-install already share, before it writes its ledger, and holds
    it until it exits (carlos-emr/carlos#3678). Previously the importer
    only became visible to those paths' o19 guard once its ledger was
    published, leaving a window in which a configure or boot repair could
    restart MariaDB, rewrite grants or run Flyway under the copy. Every
    mode takes it (a real run, --resume, --cleanup, --dry-run); an
    import started while a provisioning run holds it is refused with
    nothing written.
  * o19-preflight takes the same lock for its staging restore, which runs
    in the clinic's MariaDB like a real run's (--write-standalone takes
    none). The lock file is opened without following a symlink and must
    be a regular file; anything else is refused with nothing written.
  * The dev snapshot version: release builds stamp the tag's version.

